lesson: 245
title: Shopping sums
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A six-pack holds 6 cans, and a dollar is worth 100 cents.
explain: To find the price of one can, turn the price of the six-pack into cents and share it among the 6 cans.
explain: A six-pack for $3.60 costs 360 cents, so one can costs 360 ÷ 6 = 60 cents.
vars: a=name b=name x=1..6 y=0..9 z=2..9
let: p = x * 100 + y * 10
answer: p / 6
exercise: A six-pack of juice costs ${x}.{y}0. How many cents does one can cost?
exercise: {a} buys a six-pack of lemonade for ${x}.{y}0. What is the price of one can in cents?
exercise: At the supermarket a six-pack of cola is ${x}.{y}0. If the cans were sold one by one at the same price, how many cents would one can cost?
exercise: {a} paid ${x}.{y}0 for a six-pack of sparkling water. How many cents did each can cost?
exercise: A six-pack of iced tea is on sale for ${x}.{y}0. How much is one can, in cents?
exercise: six-pack of soda ${x}.{y}0, how many cents a can then
exercise: {a} got a six pack of root beer for ${x+1}.{y}0 — no sorry ${x}.{y}0. cents per can?
exercise: grabbed a six-pack for the picnic, ${x}.{y}0, it was boiling hot lol. how much each can in cents
exercise: {b} says a ${x}.{y}0 six-pack means each can is {p/3} cents. hmm is that right? cents per can
exercise: {a}'s dad bought a six-pack of ginger ale for ${x}.{y}0, how much a can, cents
exercise: six pack of energy drinks ${x}.{y}0 at the gas station y'all, cents each?
test: How many cents is one can worth when a six-pack sells for ${x}.{y}0?
test: {a} splits the cost of a ${x}.{y}0 six-pack of juice so that each can has its own price. What is that price in cents?
test: A six-pack of milkshakes costs ${x}.{y}0 and a bag of ice costs ${z}. How many cents does one milkshake cost?
test: {a} and {b} share a six-pack of lemonade bought for ${x}.{y}0. What is one can's share of the price, in cents?
test: six-pack ${x}.{y}0, whats one can in cents
test: my mom says a six-pack at ${x}.{y}0 works out to how many cents a can? help
exam: Determine the unit price, expressed in cents, of a single can, given that a six-pack is offered for sale at ${x}.{y}0.
exam: It is given that {a} acquires a six-pack of beverages for the sum of {x} dollars and {y*10} cents. Ascertain the cost attributable to each can, in cents.
exam: A six-pack having been purchased for ${x}.{y}0, state the price per can in cents, on the assumption that the cost is apportioned equally.
exam: Whereas a six-pack of juice is priced at ${x}.{y}0, compute the cost, in cents, of one constituent can thereof.
exercise: Dad's between jobs, so {a} checks every price: a six-pack of juice costs ${x}.{y}0. How many cents is one can?
exercise: the shop's adjusted its prices again, robbing gits. a six-pack of cola is ${x}.{y}0 now. how many cents a can?
test: {a}'s older brother was economical with the truth: he said one can cost a dollar, but the six-pack was only ${x}.{y}0. What did one can really cost, in cents?
